  <!-- Reader prerequisite contract added during the prerequisite audit. -->
  <preface xml:id="how-to-read">
    <title>How to Read This Book</title>
    <p>
      This book assumes algebra, trigonometry, and single-variable calculus: limits,
      continuity, derivatives and their product and chain rules, the Mean Value Theorem,
      definite integrals, the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, and basic substitution.
      It does not assume prior multivariable calculus, differential geometry, or exterior algebra.
      Vectors and the linear algebra used in the book are developed in Chapters 1<ndash/>3.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read sections in the order presented. A worked example may introduce a new idea before
      its formal definition; the explanation supplies the tools needed for the following
      checkpoints. Looking-ahead remarks motivate later topics and do not add prerequisites
      to the current exercises. When a theorem's proof is deferred, its statement may be used
      under its stated hypotheses, but the forward reference is not a proof already given.
      The appendices are references, not additional assumed background unless a passage
      explicitly directs you to the needed explanation.
    </p>
  </preface>
</frontmatter>
